.TH NOG 1 "August 2026" "nog v1.3.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
nog \- tier-aware package manager for KognogOS
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nog
.I <command>
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ packages ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nog
is the package manager for KognogOS. It wraps
.BR pacman (8)
\(em and optionally an AUR helper such as
.BR yay (8)
or
.BR paru (8)
\(em with a three-tier update management system, giving every package on the
system a protection level based on how critical it is.
.PP
Tier 1 packages (the kernel, bootloader, glibc, systemd, mesa) are held for
30 days after upstream publish before auto-releasing. Tier 2 packages are
held for 15 days. Tier 3 packages are held for 7 days. Expert users can
switch Tier 1 to a manual-sign-off mode via
.B tier-pins.toml
if they prefer to eyeball every kernel upgrade personally. See
.B TIER SYSTEM
for the full model.
.PP
.B nog
should always be run as your regular user. It escalates to root only where
genuinely required — by invoking
.B sudo pacman
for package transactions and
.B sudo tee
for writing
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml .
See the
.B PRIVILEGES AND SUDO
section below for the complete privilege model.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B nog install \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...]
Install one or more packages. Tier classification is printed as informational
output; installs are never blocked or gated — explicit user commands execute
user intent. If an AUR helper is configured (see
.BR nog.conf (5)),
the install routes through the helper, so AUR-only packages work transparently
without any pre-check. Tier protection applies to future updates, not to installs.
.TP
.B nog remove \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...]
Remove one or more packages via
.BR pacman (8).
Works uniformly for packages originally installed from official repos or AUR.
.TP
.B nog update
Compute a tier-aware system upgrade plan and hand each source to the tool
that owns it \(em
.BR pacman (8)
for the official repositories, then the configured AUR helper, then
.BR flatpak (1)
and
.BR snap (8).
Every pending upgrade is shown before any transaction starts, classified into
one of three buckets:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B Ready to install
\(em hold window has expired; safe to upgrade
.IP \(bu 2
.B Held
\(em still within the tier's hold window, or Tier 1 package with
.B manual_signoff = true
.IP \(bu 2
.B Unknown
\(em no usable build date (locally built, disabled repo, helper lookup
failed, or a sync-DB entry that doesn't match the pending candidate's version)
.RE
Each
.B Unknown
package triggers an interactive
.B "[y/N]"
prompt. Pending official repo upgrades come from
.BR checkupdates (8);
pending AUR upgrades come from
.B "<helper> -Qua" .
Build dates come from the fresh sync-database snapshot
.BR checkupdates (8)
itself just synced (its private dbpath: \fB$CHECKUPDATES_DB\fR, default
\fB${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/checkup-db-<uid>/\fR) \(em the same snapshot that produced
the candidate list \(em and, for AUR packages not found in any sync DB, from
the helper's cached metadata (\fB<helper> -Sai\fR). If the snapshot is
missing, nog falls back to \fB/var/lib/pacman/sync\fR with a warning; holds
may then be dated from stale build dates. A hold is only ever dated from a DB
entry matching the pending candidate's exact version \(em mismatches bucket
as Unknown.
The handoff runs in four steps, skipping any source with nothing cleared.
.B pacman -Syu
upgrades the official repositories with
.B --ignore=
listing all held and user-skipped packages; the helper is then invoked as
.B "<helper> -S"
with the cleared AUR package names only, so a held AUR package is never named
and cannot move. Flatpak and snap follow, unchanged.
.PP
Failure handling differs by step. If
.B pacman
fails, nog cancels without touching another source \(em AUR packages are
compiled against official libraries and must not be built on a system whose
repository upgrade did not complete. If a later step fails, nog reports it and
asks whether to continue, defaulting to
.BR no ;
a non-interactive run stops.
.PP
Every run is appended to a per-day CSV log
.RB ( "YYYYMMDD nog-update.csv"
under
.BR "[paths] run_logs" ,
default
.IR ~/.local/share/nog/logs )
mirroring the report tables plus the run's outcome
.RB ( installed ,
.BR cancelled ,
.BR "all held" ,
.BR "up to date" ,
.BR "pacman handoff did not complete" ,
.BR "installed with incomplete steps: ..." );
logs older than 90 days are pruned automatically. Logging soft-fails with a
warning and never blocks an update.
.PP
Before the buckets render,
.B nog update
checks installed kernel and
.IR <kernel>-headers
versions for every Tier 1 kernel. If any pair disagrees
(e.g.
.IR linux-lts
at 6.18.22-1 but
.IR linux-lts-headers
at 6.18.29-1, the fingerprint of a v1.0.2-style desync), a red warning block
is printed naming each desynced pair and pointing at
.BR --realign .
.TP
.B nog update \-\-realign
Recover from a kernel / headers version mismatch. For each desynced kernel
whose pending upgrade version matches the installed headers version, the row
is pulled from the
.B Held
bucket into the
.B Ready
bucket with the annotation
.RB [ "Tier 1 · realigned to match installed headers" ].
The subsequent transaction upgrades the kernel to match the headers in a
single coherent step, after which the next DKMS rebuild succeeds. If no held
kernel matches the installed headers version, a clean fall-through notice is
printed and
.B nog update
proceeds with the standard plan.
.TP
.B nog search \fIquery\fR
Search for packages matching
.IR query
via
.BR pacman (8).
Each result is annotated by tier using the Catppuccin Mocha palette
(red Tier 1, yellow Tier 2, green Tier 3).
.TP
.B nog pin \fIpackage\fR \fB--tier=\fR\fI<1|2|3>\fR
Pin a package to a specific tier. The change is written to
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
via
.B sudo tee
and survives reboots. Pinning to Tier 3 removes any existing Tier 1 or Tier 2
entry for that package (Tier 3 is the implicit default for anything not listed).
.TP
.B nog unlock \fIpackage\fR [\fB--promote\fR]
Without
.BR --promote ,
this command is informational only. Tier 1 packages under
.B manual_signoff = true
are held wholesale; there is no per-session unlock state to toggle, so this
mode simply prints the held status and points at
.BR --promote .
With
.BR --promote ,
force-upgrade the package immediately via
.BR pacman (8)
or the configured helper, bypassing the hold window and any sign-off policy.
This is the escape hatch for a held Tier 1 package.
.TP
.B nog deactivate \fIsource\fR
Source kill switch (v1.0.9; \fBflatpak\fR v1.1.0, \fBsnap\fR v1.2.0). Valid
sources:
.BR aur ,
.BR chaotic-aur ,
.BR flatpak ,
and
.BR snap .
Deactivating
.B flatpak
persists a flag in
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
so
.B nog update
neither queries nor applies flatpak updates; installed apps stay installed
but frozen. The flatpak binary itself is never touched.
Deactivating
.B aur
persists a flag in
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
and turns off every AUR-aware path at once — update detection, install
routing, and the upgrade handoff (which then runs through
.BR pacman (8)
only, so foreign packages cannot move). The configured helper in
.I nog.conf
is left untouched and resumes on activation. Deactivating
.B chaotic-aur
comments the \fB[chaotic-aur]\fR section out of
.I /etc/pacman.conf
(timestamped backup first, marker \fB#nog#\fR), then refreshes the sync
databases: no tool on the system can resolve from the repository until it is
reactivated, and installed chaotic packages stay installed but frozen.
Intended for incident response — e.g. an active AUR supply-chain attack.
.TP
.B nog activate \fIsource\fR
Reverse of
.BR "nog deactivate" .
Restores the AUR paths, or uncomments exactly the pacman.conf lines nog
disabled (hand-written comments survive byte-for-byte) and refreshes the
databases.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help information.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information.
.
.SH FLATPAK SUPPORT
Since v1.1.0
.B nog update
also reports and applies Flatpak app updates, under the same tier hold
windows as everything else. The hold clock for a flatpak ref is the
publish date of the pending remote commit (from
.BR "flatpak remote-info" ),
the analogue of a package's build date. Flatpak refs classify as Tier 3 by
default and can be pinned like any package with
.BR "nog pin" .
Rows carry a
.B "\(bu flatpak"
marker in the Note column so the source of every update is visible before
you confirm.
.PP
Flatpak is an optional backend: if the
.B flatpak
binary is absent the source is simply dormant, never an error. Because
flatpak has no
.B \-\-ignore
option, nog enforces holds by naming: it passes exactly the refs it cleared
this run and nothing else. A failed remote query is reported and the run
continues with flatpak untouched \(em never assumed quiet.
.
.SH SNAP SUPPORT
Since v1.2.0
.B nog update
also reports and refreshes Snap packages, under the same tier hold windows.
The hold clock for a snap is the publish date of the pending revision in the
snap's tracked channel, read from
.BR "snap info" .
Rows carry a
.B "\(bu snap"
marker in the Note column.
.PP
snapd is packaged only in the AUR on Arch, so nog never requires it: without
the
.B snap
binary the source is dormant and silent, never an error. Unlike every other
nog operation,
.B "snap refresh"
requires root \(em nog stays unprivileged and escalates through
.BR sudo (8)
for that step alone. Holds are enforced the same way as flatpak: nog names
exactly the snaps it cleared this run and nothing else.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/nog/nog.conf
Main nog configuration file. Defines paths, hold windows (\fB[holds]\fR), and
AUR helper preference (\fB[aur]\fR).
.TP
.I /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
Tier assignments for all managed packages. Edited by
.B nog pin
(written via
.BR "sudo tee" )
or manually by the system administrator.
.B manual_signoff
under
.B [tier1]
controls whether Tier 1 auto-releases (default) or stays wholesale held
(expert mode).
.TP
.I /var/lib/pacman/sync/*.db
Pacman sync databases. Read-only by nog, for build-date lookup during
.BR "nog update" .
.TP
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
Source kill-switch state, managed by
.B nog activate
/
.BR "nog deactivate" ;
written via
.BR "sudo tee" .
A missing file or missing key means the source is active. An unreadable file
fails CLOSED — every source is treated as deactivated until the file is
rewritten by an activate/deactivate command.
.TP
.I /etc/pacman.conf
Parsed for enabled repositories. Written only by
.B nog activate/deactivate chaotic-aur
(section toggle with a \fB#nog#\fR marker), always preceded by a timestamped
backup (\fIpacman.conf.nog-bak-<stamp>\fR).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nog/logs/
Per-run CSV update logs (\fBYYYYMMDD nog-update.csv\fR), one row per pending
package per run plus the run's outcome. Location configurable via
.B [paths] run_logs
in
.IR nog.conf ;
files older than 90 days are pruned after each run.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/nog
The nog binary.
.
.SH TIER SYSTEM
Every package nog manages is classified into one of three tiers, defined in
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml .
Each tier has a hold window (from the
.B [holds]
section of
.IR /etc/nog/nog.conf )
that must elapse after the package's upstream publish date before the update is
applied during
.BR "nog update" .
.SS Tier 1 \(em 30-day hold, auto-release by default
The most critical packages: kernel, bootloader, glibc, systemd, mesa. By default
(\fBmanual_signoff = false\fR), Tier 1 auto-releases after a 30-day hold window
\(em a full month of community testing before an update lands on your system.
.PP
Expert mode: set
.B manual_signoff = true
under
.B [tier1]
in
.IR tier-pins.toml
to switch Tier 1 to always-held; updates release only via
.B "nog unlock <pkg> --promote" .
Recommended only for users who want to review every kernel / glibc / systemd
upgrade personally.
.PP
Default Tier 1 packages:
.BR linux ", " linux-zen ", " linux-lts ", " linux-hardened ", " systemd ", "
.BR systemd-libs ", " glibc ", " grub ", " efibootmgr ", " mkinitcpio ", "
.BR pacman ", " mesa .
.SS Tier 2 \(em 15-day hold
Key desktop applications and system services. Held for 15 days after upstream
publish \(em enough time for major regressions to surface without falling far behind.
.PP
Default Tier 2 packages:
.BR plasma-meta ", " plasma-desktop ", " sddm ", " pipewire ", " pipewire-pulse ", "
.BR wireplumber ", " networkmanager ", " firefox ", " dolphin ", " konsole ", "
.BR kate ", " grubforge ", " alacritty ", " fish ", " alacrittyforge .
.SS Tier 3 \(em 7-day hold
Everything else. A short 7-day safety buffer without meaningful delay. Anything
not listed in Tier 1 or Tier 2 falls into Tier 3 automatically.
.SS Coupling \(em \fI<X>-headers\fR follows its kernel
A kernel package and its matching
.IR <name>-headers
package share a single PKGBUILD upstream, which means they share a single
build date and must be installed at the same version on disk \(em every DKMS
hook (e.g.
.BR nvidia-open-dkms (1))
rebuilds against
.IR <kernel>-headers
and places its output under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/<KVER>/ ,
which is only present when
.IR <kernel>
itself is installed at the matching version.
.PP
.B nog
auto-couples this relationship: any package matching the
.IR <X>-headers
pattern is classified as Tier 1 when
.IR <X>
is Tier 1. This is hardcoded behavior, not configurable. The Arch naming
convention is universal and the failure mode (broken DKMS module, unbound
GPU after reboot) is severe enough that opting out is not supported.
.SS Groups (advanced)
The optional
.B [groups]
table in
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
is the escape hatch for non-standard kernel names whose headers do not match
the
.IR <X>-headers
pattern (e.g.
.IR linux-cachyos-cacule-headers ),
or for bundling extra packages into a kernel's tier.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[groups]
cachyos-bundle = [
    "linux-cachyos",
    "linux-cachyos-headers",
    "linux-cachyos-cacule-headers",
]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every member of a group inherits the highest tier present among any other
member of the same group.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Install a package (nog prompts for sudo itself if needed):
.B nog install htop
.TP
Update the system:
.B nog update
.TP
Search with tier annotations:
.B nog search firefox
.TP
Pin a package to Tier 2:
.B nog pin htop --tier=2
.TP
Force-upgrade a held Tier 1 package:
.B nog unlock linux-zen --promote
.
.SH PRIVILEGES AND SUDO
.B nog
itself always runs as the invoking user; it is not designed to be run with
.BR sudo .
If you prefix
.B sudo nog
while an AUR helper is configured, nog detects the invocation (via
.B $SUDO_USER
or
.BR $SUDO_UID )
and exits with an error, because
.BR yay (8)
and
.BR paru (8)
refuse to run as root.
.PP
nog escalates to root in exactly two places, both as transparent
.BR sudo (8)
subprocess invocations:
.TP
.B sudo pacman -S | -R | -Syu ...
For package transactions during
.BR install ,
.BR remove ,
.BR update ,
and
.B "unlock --promote"
\(em but only when no AUR helper is configured. When a helper is configured,
nog hands the transaction to the helper (as the invoking user), and the
helper runs its own
.B sudo pacman
internally for the pacman step.
.TP
.B sudo tee /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
For the single file
.B nog
ever writes. Used only during
.BR "nog pin" .
The new file contents are rebuilt in memory and piped through
.BR tee ;
nog itself never runs with root privileges.
.PP
.B nog
never modifies any other file on your system \(em not
.IR /etc/pacman.conf ,
not
.IR /etc/pacman.d/ ,
not
.IR /var/lib/pacman/local/ ,
not the pacman keyring, and not
.IR /etc/sudoers .
It is a thin wrapper over
.BR pacman (8),
.BR yay (8),
or
.BR paru (8);
every install, remove, and upgrade is subject to pacman's signature
verification and conflict resolution, which nog cannot bypass.
.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS DKMS rebuild fails after \fBnog update\fR
If a DKMS hook (typically
.BR nvidia-open-dkms ,
.BR nvidia-dkms ,
or
.BR virtualbox-host-dkms )
emits
.RS
.PP
.B "ERROR: Missing <KVER> kernel modules tree for module <name>/<version>."
.RE
.PP
your kernel and its headers have diverged on disk \(em headers are at
.IR <KVER>
but the kernel binary for that version isn't installed under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/<KVER>/ .
.PP
In v1.0.3 and later this state is detected automatically; the desync warning
block in
.B nog update
points at the recovery flag:
.PP
.RS
.B nog update --realign
.RE
.PP
Manual recovery (use when
.B --realign
cannot apply, e.g. headers are ahead of any pending kernel upgrade):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo pacman -S linux-zen linux-lts      # adjust to your kernels
sudo pacman -S nvidia-open-dkms          # or the failing DKMS package
.fi
.RE
.PP
Verify the rebuild with
.B dkms status
and look for the expected
.I .ko.zst
files under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/$(uname\ -r)/updates/dkms/ .
.SS Verifying coupling is active
.B nog search linux-zen-headers
should annotate the result with
.RB [ "Tier 1 \(em 30d hold" ].
A green Tier 3 annotation means you are still running v1.0.2 or earlier;
upgrade nog before the next
.BR "nog update" .
.
.SS \fBinstalling <A> breaks dependency '<lib>.so=N' required by <B>\fR
pacman refuses the whole transaction and nothing installs. A package whose hold
has expired bumps a shared library version, while a package still linking the
old one is inside its window; nog cleared one and held the other, and pacman
cannot split the pair.
.PP
Nothing is broken \(em pacman caught it and declined. Move the pair forward
together with
.BR "nog install <A> <B>" ,
which installs both in one transaction. Never downgrade the cleared package
back; the safe direction is forward, onto the version the repositories already
carry. Waiting also works: the held package releases on its own schedule and
the wall disappears with it.
.PP
nog does not yet detect this before handing off.
.SS pacman's log shows only one ignored package
.B nog
passes a single comma-joined
.B \-\-ignore
argument. pacman splits that string in place inside its own
.IR argv ,
then writes its
.RB \(dq Running\ '...' \(dq
line, so the log records the first name and drops the rest \(em one name can
mean a hundred and sixty were passed. The holds were applied correctly; the
.RB \(dq "warning: <pkg>: ignoring package upgrade" \(dq
lines are the accurate record. A pacman logging artefact, not a nog defect.
